        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-141615",
        ":question/problem": "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}-12x+28.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+28\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $28$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+28\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-8.\n$$"
      },
