        ":question/problem": "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-10y+53.$\n\n${y}^{2}-10y+53=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+53\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $53$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+53\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+53\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+28.\n$$"
