        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-250517",
        ":question/problem": "If the expression ${x}^{2}-10x$ is written as $(x-p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+0\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $0$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-25\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=5$ and $q=-25.$"
