        ":question/problem": "If the expression ${x}^{2}-2x$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1{)}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1{)}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=-1.$"
      },
