        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-141622",
        ":question/problem": "If the expression ${y}^{2}-10y+30$ is written as $(y+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$"
